    "q-105207": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105207",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-2,4]\\to [-3,9]$ which is given by $f(x)=2x+1?$\n\n- $f(-2)=f(4)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [-2,4],$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection",
      "worked_solution": "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114612/assets/5fd931a87d6a5fb32d10ed931f443a8175f0760eba9fe2fe69336f8f79e9fd68.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $f(2)=2(-2)+1=-3$ and $f(4)=2\\cdot 4+1=9.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $[-2,4]$ that map to the same element in $[-3,9].$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-2,4],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
